Answers the one question #103's whole design forks on: can the Chromium GPU
process, AFTER LowerToken(), still reach displayxr-service?

  pipe opens AND xrCreateInstance succeeds -> BRANCH A  (~2 patches, no broker,
                                                         no runtime change)
  pipe opens, create fails                 -> BRANCH A' (+1 small runtime
                                                         change for R2)
  pipe blocked                             -> BRANCH B  (4 browser patches +
                                                         4 runtime changes,
                                                         incl. an IPC wire
                                                         change)

The issue and 0071's comment both assert the pipe is unreachable post-sandbox.
The runtime source says the opposite: the pipe's security descriptor grants AC
(AppContainer) and RC (restricted code) GRGWGX against an object labelled Low
integrity (ipc_server_mainloop_windows.cpp:120-129 — that is exactly a Chromium
GPU process's token), and there are ZERO registry reads under
src/xrt/ipc/client/. The belief predates runtime#743's pipe work and appears
never to have been re-tested.

TWO PROBES, both from inside SubmitBatchSync — Viz's present sequence, hundreds
of frames after the sandbox engaged, so "post-LowerToken" is not an assumption:

  1. CreateFileA on the runtime pipe, logging the handle or GetLastError().
     The name was read out of the runtime source rather than trusted from prose;
     both ends agree on it:
       client: src/xrt/ipc/client/ipc_client_connection.c:554
       server: src/xrt/ipc/server/ipc_server_mainloop_windows.cpp:328
       "\\.\pipe\displayxr\displayxr_comp_ipc"
     Same access flags the real client uses (:372). The log interprets the three
     answers that matter: ACCESS_DENIED = Branch B; PIPE_BUSY = the access check
     PASSED (Branch A); FILE_NOT_FOUND = the service is not running, re-run.

  2. A full second xrCreateInstance via the REAL init helpers
     (EnumerateInstanceExtensions + CreateWeaveInstance), so the probe tests the
     same extension list the real create would — a probe with a different list
     proves nothing. Tried with the rig set, then weave-only, which separates
     "the loader and the pipe refused us" from "only the rig refused", i.e.
     R2 from the connection. Destroyed immediately.

     Knowingly accepted for the probe's lifetime: the runtime holds
     `static volatile uintptr_t g_live_instance` (oxr_api_instance.c:43), a
     one-live-instance-per-process assumption, and a second live instance makes
     xrPollEvent on the FIRST return XR_EVENT_UNAVAILABLE. The weave client
     never calls xrPollEvent, so it is harmless for this consumer — but it is a
     documented assumption being violated on purpose, which is why the probe
     destroys what it creates on the spot and why this patch never merges.

FIRING. The design asks for %TEMP% trigger files
(displayxr_e0_pipe_probe / displayxr_e0_create_probe) and they are here, but as
the SECONDARY trigger: a %TEMP% existence check is itself something the sandbox
may refuse, and a probe that never fires proves nothing. The PRIMARY trigger is
automatic — both probes run once at batch frame 300 (~5 s), needing no
filesystem at all. Whether the %TEMP% check was even reachable is logged as its
own datum, since that is useful to know independently.

All output is LOG(ERROR) tagged [DisplayXR][E0] so it lands without --v.

HOW TO RUN: build this branch on the box, install, launch the browser by
double-clicking the icon with --log-file, open an inline-3D page, wait ~10 s,
then grep the log for "[DisplayXR][E0]". Attach the slice to #103. Re-fire under
other conditions (service stopped / just restarted) by touching the trigger
files, if the reachable= line says the check works.

NOT FOR MERGE. Never rebase this onto main; it exists to be built once and
deleted. 0077 (the reconnect ladder) is gated on what it reports.

Refs: DisplayXR/displayxr-browser#103
